course: dictionary
lesson: 13597
title: Inconvenience and polite words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a word is used to sound polite. Inconvenience is one of these words. It means trouble or difficulty that makes things harder than they should be. «We apologise for any inconvenience» is a polite way of saying, "We know this is annoying for you."
explain: Read the words around it for clues. «The broken lift is an inconvenience» tells you the lift does not work and that this causes small trouble. «Trains are late, so we apologise for any inconvenience» tells you the trouble is for the passengers. Ask yourself: what kind of trouble would fit here?
explain: Inconvenience can also be a verb, which means to cause someone trouble or extra work. «I don't want to inconvenience you» means I do not want to make things hard for you. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "I don't want to cause you trouble." It still makes sense.
fact: «Inconvenience» means trouble or difficulty that makes things less easy.
fact: As a verb, «inconvenience» means to cause someone trouble or extra work.
fact: «Sorry for the inconvenience» is a polite phrase used when something has gone wrong.
fact: «A minor inconvenience» means a small trouble that is not serious.
fact: Inconvenience is often a soft, polite word for real trouble, so a notice may use it instead of a bigger word.
exercise: «The broken lift is an inconvenience.» What does inconvenience mean here? | A kind present | A big party for everyone | *Something that causes small trouble | A fast journey
exercise: A notice says: «We apologise for any inconvenience while the road is closed.» Why do they apologise? | Because the road has been painted bright red | Because the road is brand new and shiny | *Because the closed road causes trouble for drivers | Because they are very happy about the road
exercise: «I don't want to inconvenience you.» Find and copy the word that means to cause someone trouble. | you | want | *inconvenience | sorry
test: «Sorry for the inconvenience» is said when... | *Something has gone a bit wrong and caused trouble | Someone has won a prize | The weather is sunny and warm | The shop is giving things away for free
test: What does «a minor inconvenience» suggest? | *A small trouble that is not serious | A long holiday by the sea | A huge disaster that stops everything | A happy surprise
exam: Which sentence uses inconvenience as a verb? | «Sorry for the inconvenience.» | «The inconvenience was small.» | «It was a minor inconvenience.» | *«The delay will inconvenience many passengers.»
